e the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x & =-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x & =-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2} & =-5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2})-9 & =-5 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-9 & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}-9 & =-5 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =-5+9 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, $p=3$ and $q=4$. Now, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x+3| & =2 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-3+2=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3-2=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411 true] [17592186068498 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x & =-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x & =-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2} & =-5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2})-9 & =-5 \\\\ (x+3{)}^{2}-9 & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3{)}^{2}-9 & =-5 \\\\ (x+3{)}^{2} & =-5+9 \\\\ (x+3{)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, $p=3$ and $q=4.$ Now, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3{)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x+3| & =2 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-3+2=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3-2=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536886 17592186576411 false] [17592186068499 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\